    <!-- About -->
    <sys:String x:Key="L.About_Desc">Reads the mods installed via Mod Organizer 2, lets you batch-assign BodySlide slider sets into groups, and generates group files in BodySlide's native format. Works with any BodySlide-supported game such as Skyrim SE/AE and Fallout 4.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.About_AuthorLabel">Author</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.About_Compat">The group files are fully compatible with BodySlide; each group becomes its own file named after the group, stored in BodySlide's SliderGroups folder.</sys:String>

    <!-- Help -->
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_H1">1. Common tasks</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_01">This tool reads the mods installed via Mod Organizer 2 (MO2), lets you batch-assign outfits (BodySlide slider sets) into groups, and generates group files in BodySlide's native format, one file per group.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_02">1. Pick the MO2 instance and Profile — global instances and portable installs are discovered automatically; the Profile defaults to the last one used.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_03">2. Confirm the BodySlide folder — auto-detection usually succeeds. The grey "effective project path" line below the cards is where BodySlide actually reads outfits and groups; keep an eye on it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_04">3. Create a group and tick outfits — on the right, "⋯" → "New group" creates and selects a group (when the list is empty there is also a "New group" button in the middle of the panel); in the tree on the left tick a separator (= select every mod under it) or expand and tick single outfits. Nothing is written until you click "Add to group" in the middle; "Remove from group" undoes what you ticked.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_05">4. Save — click "Save group files" at the bottom, or use File → Save group files (Ctrl+S). Each group becomes one file named after the group (e.g. UBE.xml). Afterwards restart BodySlide (if you launch it through MO2, restart MO2 too).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_H2">2. Quick start (4 steps)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_S2_Top">[Top settings area]</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_06">MO2 instance: global instances and portable installs under %LOCALAPPDATA%\ModOrganizer are found automatically; "Select instance folder…" points to an instance folder (containing ModOrganizer.ini) manually; "Refresh" rescans instances.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_07">Profile: lists every Profile available to that instance. The enabled-mod list comes from that Profile's modlist.txt, in the same order as MO2's left pane.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_08">BodySlide: searches enabled mods for folders containing BodySlide*.exe and Config.xml; if none is found, use "Browse…" to point at one.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_09">Output location: decides where the group files are written (see section 6). The right side of the status bar shows the current output folder — hover it for the full path.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_S2_Tree">[Left mod tree]</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_10">Hierarchy: separator (grey) → mod (bold, collapsible) → outfits; same order as MO2's left pane, showing only enabled mods that contain BodySlide outfits.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_11">The two filter boxes at the top do different jobs: "Filter outfit names…" matches outfit names only, "Filter mod names…" matches mod names only (contiguous substring, case-insensitive; a mod-name hit shows all of that mod's outfits). "Unassigned only" hides outfits that are already in a group.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_12">A tick only marks what you want to act on — nothing is written until you click "Add to group" / "Remove from group" in the middle. Ticking a mod or a separator ticks all of its outfits.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_13">The mark before an outfit name means it is already in the currently selected group; the "in group x/total" badge to the right of a mod title shows how many of its outfits are in that group.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_S2_Groups">[Right group panel]</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_14">Group list: click to select (the ticks and marks on the left follow along without losing your scroll position), double-click to preview its members. New / rename / view / delete and import all live under "..." in the header, and are also on the list's right-click menu; "Undo" and "Rule grouping" are the two buttons next to it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_15">The "Rule grouping" button (next to "Undo") opens a panel at the bottom of this page: batch work by keyword - see section 5.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_16">Undo (Ctrl+Z, or Edit → Undo): rolls back the last 30 grouping actions — see section 6.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_17">File → Import group files…: merge other group XMLs into the current list and keep editing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_18">New-mod reminder: when a scan finds newly installed outfit mods a popup lists them by separator → mod → outfit (same as the main tree, expandable) — tick, pick a target group, click "Add selected" (undoable), and you can repeat with different target groups. For long lists use the two filter boxes above (left filters outfit names, right filters mod / separator names); ticks survive filtering. Closing defers it; you can still find those outfits later via "Unassigned only".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_S2_Status">[Status bar]</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_19">Left: statistics (mods / outfits / assigned / unassigned). "Diagnostics" lives under the Tools menu; the run log is a collapsible summary line under the save button — click it to expand; once expanded it is colour-coded by level, copyable, clearable, and resizable by dragging.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_20">Right: the current output folder; hover for the full path.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_H3">3. What is on screen</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_21">On save, each group becomes its own file named after the group (e.g. UBE.xml), containing BodySlide's native &lt;SliderGroups&gt; format (UTF-8 with BOM), fully compatible with BodySlide.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_22">Saving again overwrites same-named group files and automatically cleans up leftovers from renamed/deleted groups via the internal manifest. Closing the app with unsaved changes shows a warning.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_23">Note: if a custom output folder is not somewhere BodySlide reads (the SliderGroups of the effective project path, or of a mod), BodySlide will not load those groups.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_H4">4. Saving and file layout</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_24">With large outfit counts, use keywords to round up a whole batch at once:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_25">1. Select the target group on the right first; the "Rule grouping" button in the Groups header (or Ctrl+3) opens the rule drawer at the bottom of this page with that group already filled in as the target.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_26">2. Three kinds of keywords (separate several with ;, any hit counts; case-insensitive substring match):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_27">Mod keywords: filter candidate mods by mod name first, then search outfits within them — same-named outfits in other mods are not pulled in; empty = any mod.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_28">Outfit keywords: match by outfit name; empty = all outfits.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_29">Excluded keywords: outfits whose name matches are dropped.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_30">3. "Unassigned outfits only" leaves already-grouped ones alone; the direction is "add to" or "remove from" the group.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_31">4. The preview shows separator -> mod -> matched outfits (same hierarchy as the main tree) with a live "N outfit(s) matched" count; click "Apply this rule" to execute - Ctrl+Z undoes it. Example: mod keyword ube, exclude localisation, unassigned only -> pulls all ungrouped UBE outfits into the target group in one go (outfit names need not contain ube).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_H5">5. Rule grouping (batch grouping and presets)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Presets">5. Click "Save preset" on conditions you use often and pick them up next time; "Apply all presets" runs every preset in order (the same path as "Group by presets" in the new-mods popup). Saving under a different name keeps the original and creates a second preset.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_32">Undo (Ctrl+Z, or Edit → Undo) rolls back the last 30 grouping actions one step at a time: add/remove, rule grouping, new/rename/delete group, import, and removals made in the member list. Saving to disk does not clear the undo history, but restarting the app does.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_33">Five output modes:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_34">Auto (recommended): writes to the effective project path's SliderGroups when it is a real folder; when it is CalienteTools\BodySlide under MO2's virtual Data (the most common case), writes to the MO2-dedicated mod "SliderSorter Output".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_35">MO2-dedicated mod: mods\SliderSorter Output\CalienteTools\BodySlide\SliderGroups\ — visible in MO2, toggleable per Profile, survives BodySlide reinstalls.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_36">BodySlide app folder / real game Data / custom: writes to the SliderGroups of the respective location.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_H6">6. Undo and output location</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_37">· Ticked outfits but nothing happened? A tick only selects; you still have to click "Add to group" in the middle.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_38">· New groups not showing in BodySlide? 1) Restart BodySlide (with MO2, restart MO2 too); 2) open Tools → Diagnostics and compare the "effective project path" with your "output folder" — BodySlide only reads groups from the effective project path's SliderGroups.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_39">· Why are some mods missing? The tree only shows enabled mods containing BodySlide outfits; disabled mods or mods without outfits do not appear.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_40">· (name conflict) after an outfit name? That outfit name appears in several mods; ownership is displayed for the highest-priority mod, but grouping matches by name and is unaffected.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_41">· What is SliderSorter.files.txt in the output folder? The tool's manifest, used to clean up old files automatically; BodySlide ignores it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_42">· Something crashed? Exceptions are written to %APPDATA%\SliderSorter\crash.log — feel free to send that file to the developer.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_H7">7. Troubleshooting</sys:String>

    <!-- 8. Two independent results — the deliverables of one scan.
         A single scan yields two things that do not depend on each other: the groups
         (SliderGroups\*.xml) and the output-conflict choices (BuildSelection.xml).
         This chapter exists because the help previously covered only the grouping line,
         leaving "Output conflicts" with no entry in the table of contents. -->
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_H8">8. Two independent results</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Conflict_Intro">One scan produces two results that do not depend on each other, one per tab. You can do either one on its own — neither has to come first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Conflict_A">· Groups ("Grouping" tab) — assign outfits to groups, then "Save group files" writes SliderGroups\*.xml for BodySlide to read.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Conflict_B">· Output-conflict choices ("Output conflicts" tab) — when several outfits write the same body mesh file, choose which mod generates it, then "Write to BodySlide…" saves BuildSelection.xml. Choices live inside this tool until written; the original file is backed up as .bak first. It always goes to the BodySlide program folder (next to Config.xml) — that is the only place BodySlide reads it from, so it does not follow the output location in Settings.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Conflict_C">Each writes its own file and never overwrites the other: after a single scan both are available, in any order, and you can come back to either one later.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Conflict_D">"Shared output within one mod" (hundreds of recolours of the same outfit) is normal and is not counted as something to resolve; tick "Cross-mod conflicts only" to filter them out.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Toc">Contents</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_TocHint">Click a heading to jump to that section.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Task1">Put one mod's outfits into a group — tick that mod on the left (ticking the mod name selects all its outfits) → select the target group on the right → click "Add to group" in the middle → click "Save group files".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Task2">Gather similar outfits scattered across several mods - press the "Rule grouping" button on the right, then narrow them down with keywords in one pass (see section 5).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Task3">An outfit ended up in the wrong group — tick it on the left → click "Remove from group"; or just press Ctrl+Z to undo the last step.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Task4">New groups do not show up in BodySlide — restart BodySlide first (if you launch it through MO2, restart MO2 too); if they are still missing, open Tools → Diagnostics and check that the "effective project path" matches the "output dir".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help_Task5">See what is inside a group — double-click the group name, or use "⋯" → "View group" to open the member list (filterable, with batch removal).</sys:String>

